# Architecture Standards

## Sections

- **No `main-` prefix** on new sections; it is reserved for template main
  sections. Name by function: `featured-products`, `image-banner`, `testimonials`.
- Files: `sections/[name].liquid`, `assets/section-[name].css`,
  `assets/section-[name].js` — CSS and JS optional.
- An inline `<script>` is fine up to roughly 100 lines; beyond that, or once it
  is complex, it moves to `assets/section-[name].js`.

```liquid
<product-info
  data-url="{{ product.url }}"
  data-section="{{ section.id }}"
  class="color-{{ section.settings.color_scheme }} section-{{ section.id }}-padding"
>
  …
</product-info>
```

## Snippets

- Almost every snippet is prefixed `component-`, named by function:
  `component-product-card`, `component-cart-drawer`, `component-filters-sidebar`.
- Files: `snippets/component-[name].liquid`, `assets/component-[name].css`,
  `assets/component-[name].js`.

## JavaScript is a custom element

```javascript
if (!customElements.get('product-card')) {
  customElements.define(
    'product-card',
    class ProductCard extends HTMLElement {
      constructor() {
        super();
      }

      connectedCallback() {
        // Initialise when added to the DOM
      }

      disconnectedCallback() {
        // Clean up
      }
    }
  );
}
```

## Shared vs. Section-Owned — the decision test

> Is the behaviour and appearance genuinely the *same* thing in every place it
> is used, differing only in content and configuration values?
>
> - **Yes** → one shared `component-*` file, built so consumers configure it.
> - **No** → separate `section-*` files, one per section.

| Case | Answer | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Base's three product carousels — Featured Products, Related Products, Featured Collections v2 | **Separate** `section-*` files | Each wraps Swiper with different configuration, breakpoints and slide structure |
| A scroll-snap-plus-dots carousel used by several content sections | **One shared** `component-*` | Identical behaviour and markup contract; only the cards and values differ |

### Building a shared primitive so it stays shared

- Behaviour toggles via data attributes on the element — dots, snap alignment,
  items per view, loop — not branching on a page name or class.
- Appearance via CSS custom properties the consuming section sets.
- No consumer-specific names inside — no `if (page === 'about')`.

A new design that needs the primitive's internals edited is the test saying
"No": split it.

### Naming follows actual scope, not origin

A `section-*` file is loaded by one section. When something turns out to be
shared, rename it `component-*` **in the same change** that widens its scope;
`check-conventions.py` reports a `section-*` asset loaded by more than one section.
